se are “just a bigger version” of your home inkjet—they’re different technologies that just happen to look similar on the outside because paper goes in one end and comes out the other. --- ### 2. Even if they *were* using an office inkjet, the economics would be different Suppose the community paper genuinely uses a wide-format home/office inkjet printer for short runs. Even then, they wouldn’t be using retail cartridges: - They’d be using **extra high-yield cartridges or continuous ink supply systems (CISS)** with bulk ink bottles. Bulk inkjet ink can cost $20–$30 per litre instead of $3,000 per litre in retail cartridges. That brings a heavy-coverage colour page down to cents, not dollars. - They’re printing maybe 200–500 copies at a time, which still isn’t “mass production scale” but it’s 200–500 times more copies than your single newspaper. The fixed time and setup cost to fiddle with paper, clear jams, replace cartridges, etc., gets spread over those copies. You’re paying the full retail cartridge price and your own labour cost for one. --- ### 3. The paper isn’t real newsprint, but it’s cheaper than photo paper Look closely at that community paper. It might look like newspaper, but it’s almost certainly not the same low-cost, high-absorbency, thin newsprint used by a major daily. Office printers can’t reliably feed genuine newsprint—it’s too thin, dusty, and absorbent; it would jam or curl immediately. What they’re using is likely: - A **lightweight uncoated stock** (like 48–60 gsm speciality paper that’s a bit stiffer than newsprint), or - A **standard 20 lb copy paper** trimmed to tabloid size, which is more expensive than newsprint but still way cheaper than inkjet photo paper. So it might *look* like a newspaper (grainy photos, uncoated feel), but it’s a simulation, not a chemically identical product. And it’s bought in reams, not one sheet at a time. --- ### 4. The real test: ask them what it costs to print that community paper Here’s the kicker: that community paper still isn’t being sold at a profit based on the cover price (if it even has a cover price). It’s likely funded by local advertising, donations, or a municipal grant. The printing cost—even on a leased office copier—might be $0.25–$0.50 per copy for an 8-page edition, but they might distribute it for free. If you printed that same 8-page edition at home on your inkjet using retail cartridges, you’d be out $3–$10 in ink and paper, and it would take you half an hour. Scale and bulk ink purchases make all the difference. So, how different can the methods actually be? Very different—but the real lesson is that **even when the machine looks similar, the consumable supply chain is what kills you**. Office copiers use toner bought by the kilo, not ink sold by the millilitre with a printer-head tax built in. And the community paper’s “one copy” cost is hidden inside a subsidised bulk run. You, making exactly one newspaper at home, are facing the undiluted retail cost of an inefficient one-off process. So if you ever get your hands on a high-volume office colour laser copier, a ream of lightweight uncoated stock, and the willingness to print 500 copies, then yes—you could make something that looks like that community paper for a reasonable per-copy price. But shifting from "one copy on my inkjet" to "hundreds on a copier" changes everything, even though the rollers on the outside still go round and round.
